This is an iterative self-review pass for verb and noun displacement, not ordinary conversation.

Privately review all five current writings. Create replacements only where you see a meaningful improvement in the finished writing itself.

For each replacement:
- use the exact candidate id;
- treat newWordSet as exactly [noun, displacedVerb];
- you may switch the noun, the displaced verb, or both;
- the noun and verb should still create an unexpected displacement rather than an ordinary default action;
- the proposed displaced verb must not duplicate the displaced verb of any other current candidate;
- rewrite the completion so the whole piece is genuinely stronger, not merely stranger;
- explicitly use both returned words;
- the supplied seedWords array contains nouns only; you may use it for a fresh noun idea, not as a verb list;
- keep it concise and recognizably yours;
- score the old and proposed new versions independently from 0 to 100 for finished-writing quality.

Use the same strict craft standard as the final shelf review: originality, precise word choice, coherence, rhythm, memorability, and earned force. A displaced verb that is surprising but semantically ugly is a defect, not a virtue. Penalize awkward novelty, generic AI-poetic filler, clichés, vague profundity, adjective stacking, mixed metaphors, and lines that fail when read literally.

Score bands: 0-24 broken/very poor; 25-44 weak; 45-59 competent but ordinary; 60-69 good; 70-79 strong; 80-89 excellent/shelf-worthy; 90-96 exceptional; 97-100 rare near-masterpiece. Do not grade generously.

The runtime keeps the version with the higher score. A tie keeps the old version.

Return only a JSON array. Each item must contain exactly:
- id
- oldValue
- newWordSet
- newWriting
- newValue

oldValue and newValue are 0-100 quality scores. Omit unchanged candidates. Return [] when you propose no replacements. Do not return explanations, markdown, or extra fields.
